  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nHeading into Friday and the upcoming weekend, mid level ridging over \nthe western Atlantic will gradually strengthen as it pushes westward \nand continues to build over South Florida. At the surface, what is \ncurrently Tropical Depression Nine will continue to strengthen as\nit turns and moves northward towards the northern Gulf Coast \nFriday into Saturday. While this system will remain far off to the\nnorthwest and will not have any direct impacts on South Florida, \nit will help to lift the frontal boundary that has been stalled \nout over South Florida northward beginning on Friday. Winds will \nthen shift and become southeasterly on Friday, however, they will \ngradually increase heading into Friday night and Saturday as the \npressure gradient quickly tightens across the region. \n\nThe uncertainty in the forecast rises a bit heading into Friday\nand Saturday as the frontal boundary gets lifted off to the north\nof the region. Even though this front gets lifted northward, the\nenhanced south southeasterly wind flow both at the surface and\naloft could help to drag in some deep layer tropical moisture over\nthe region from the Carribean Sea. This may keep the chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms elevated across the region on Friday and\nSaturday. While the general motion of showers and thunderstorms \nwill increase during this time frame, the potential for high \nrainfall rates will continue keeping the chances for localized \nflooding in place as well. The timing this moisture increase also \nplays a role into the uncertainty as some of the guidance is a \nbit slower in bringing this area of moisture northward on Friday. \nIf this slower scenario were to play out, Friday may end up being \nmore of a typical late rainy season type day with higher chances \nof showers and storms being focused over the interior and \nSouthwest Florida instead of the entire area. In any event, the \nmain takeaway here is that even though the frontal boundary will \nbe dragged further to the north, the wet and unsettled pattern may\nlast into the first part of the weekend due to the increase in \ndeep layer tropical moisture trying to push over the region. This \nwill continue to be monitored as the week progresses.\n\nHeading into the second half of the weekend and into early next \nweek, the guidance remains in relatively good agreement with \nshowing strong mid level ridging expanding over the Florida \nPeninsula and into the Gulf during this time frame. This will \nallow for drier conditions to push into the area which will result\nin a return to more of a typical late wet season type pattern \nacross the region as winds begin to decrease and become east \nsoutheasterly. Chances of showers and thunderstorms will still \nremain in place, however, they will become more scattered in \nnature and they will favor the interior and Southwest Florida \nduring the afternoon and evening hours. High temperatures during \nthe second half of the weekend and into the early next week could\nbe above climatological normals due to the strengthening mid \nlevel ridge building into the region.",
